  "sections": {
    "BASICS": {
      "present": true,
      "status": "content",
      "content_blocks": [],
      "subsections": {
        "OVERVIEW": {
          "present": true,
          "status": "content",
          "source_heading": "OVERVIEW",
          "canonical_heading": "OVERVIEW",
          "content_blocks": [
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "The presence of excessive serum paraproteins. Paraproteins are produced by a single clone of cells and may be whole immunoglobulins, subunits, light chains, or heavy chains. Commonly seen with plasma cell neoplasms (multiple myeloma), chronic lymphocytic leukemia, or B-cell lymphoma.",
              "pdf_page": 1128,
              "printed_page": 1046,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Primary signs are related to the underlying neoplasm and could be related to bone or bone marrow invasion with neoplastic cells.",
              "pdf_page": 1128,
              "printed_page": 1046,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Markedly elevated paraprotein concentration can result in hyperviscosity syndrome (HVS).",
              "pdf_page": 1128,
              "printed_page": 1046,
              "column": 1
            }
          ]
        },
        "DEFINITION": {
          "present": false,
          "status": "missing",
          "source_heading": null,
          "canonical_heading": "DEFINITION",
          "content_blocks": []
        },
        "PATHOPHYSIOLOGY": {
          "present": false,
          "status": "missing",
          "source_heading": null,
          "canonical_heading": "PATHOPHYSIOLOGY",
          "content_blocks": []
        },
        "SYSTEMS AFFECTED": {
          "present": true,
          "status": "content",
          "source_heading": "SYSTEMS AFFECTED",
          "canonical_heading": "SYSTEMS AFFECTED",
          "content_blocks": [
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Musculoskeletal—bone lysis by neoplasia can cause pain, lameness and pathologic fractures. More common in dogs.",
              "pdf_page": 1128,
              "printed_page": 1046,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Nervous—vertebral lysis and HVS can cause neurologic abnormalities including seizures,",
              "pdf_page": 1128,
              "printed_page": 1046,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "acute blindness, paraparesis.",
              "pdf_page": 1128,
              "printed_page": 1046,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Hemic/lymphatic/immune—myelophthisis and secondary immune-mediated destruction may cause cytopenias. Paraproteins may interfere with platelet and coagulation factor function; decreased production of normal immuno globulins may increase susceptibility to",
              "pdf_page": 1128,
              "printed_page": 1046,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "infection.",
              "pdf_page": 1128,
              "printed_page": 1046,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Ophthalmic—dilated, tortuous retinal vessels, retinal detachment, or retinal",
              "pdf_page": 1128,
              "printed_page": 1046,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "hemorrhage from HVS.",
              "pdf_page": 1128,
              "printed_page": 1046,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Cardiovascular— HVS can cause hypervolemia and tachycardia, gallop rhythm, and cardiac failure (cats more",
              "pdf_page": 1128,
              "printed_page": 1046,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "than dogs).",
              "pdf_page": 1128,
              "printed_page": 1046,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Renal—failure due to antibody deposition in tubules.",
              "pdf_page": 1128,
              "printed_page": 1046,
              "column": 1
            }
          ]
        },
